      article.main
        h1 Filters
        p Filters let you use other languages in Pug templates. They take a block of plain text as an input.
        p
          | To pass options to the filter, add them inside parentheses after the filter name (just as you would do with
          a(href="attributes.html") tag attributes
          | ):
          code :less(ieCompat=false)
          | .
        p
          | All
          a(href="https://www.npmjs.com/browse/keyword/jstransformer") JSTransformer modules
          | can be used as Pug filters. Popular filters include
          code :babel
          | ,
          code :uglify-js
          | ,
          code :scss
          | , and
          code :markdown-it
          | . Check out the documentation for the JSTransformer for the options supported for the specific filter.
        p
          | If you can’t find an appropriate filter for your use case, you can write your own
          a(href="#custom-filters") custom filter
          | .
        p For example, if you want to be able to use CoffeeScript and Markdown (using Markdown-it renderer) in your Pug template, you would first make sure that these features are installed:
        pre.cm-s-default
          code
            span.cm-def $ npm
            | install
            span.cm-attribute --save
            | jstransformer-coffee-script
            span.cm-def $ npm
            | install
            span.cm-attribute --save
            | jstransformer-markdown-it
        p Now, you should be able to render the following template:
        div.preview-wrapper(data-control="interactive" data-index="0")
          div.row(data-reactroot data-reactid="1" data-react-checksum="-843964034")
            div.col-lg-6(data-reactid="2")
              div(data-reactid="3")
                textarea(data-reactid="4").
                  :markdown-it(linkify langPrefix='highlight-')
                    # Markdown
                  
                    Markdown document with http://links.com and
                  
                    ```js
                    var codeBlocks;
                    ```
                  script
                    :coffee-script
                      console.log 'This is coffee script'
            div.col-lg-6(data-reactid="5")
              div(data-reactid="6")
                textarea(readonly data-reactid="7").
                  <h1>Markdown</h1>
                  <p>Markdown document with <a href="http://links.com">http://links.com</a> and</p>
                  <pre><code class="highlight-js">var codeBlocks;
                  </code></pre>
                  <script>
                    (function() {
                      console.log('This is coffee script');
                  
                    }).call(this);
                  </script>
        div.alert.alert-warning
          h6 Warning
          p
            strong Filters are rendered at compile time.
            | This makes them fast, but it also means that they cannot support dynamic content or options.
          p By default, compilation in the browser does not have access to JSTransformer-based filters, unless the JSTransformer modules are explicitly packed and made available through a CommonJS platform (such as Browserify or Webpack). In fact, the page you are reading right now uses Browserify to make the filters available in the browser.
          p Templates pre-compiled on the server do not have this limitation.
        h2#inline-syntax
          | Inline Syntax
          a.header-anchor(href="#inline-syntax" aria-hidden="true") ¶
        p If the content of the filter is short, one can even use filters as if they are tags:
        div.preview-wrapper(data-control="interactive" data-index="1")
          div.row(data-reactroot data-reactid="1" data-react-checksum="1562366064")
            div.col-lg-6(data-reactid="2")
              div(data-reactid="3")
                textarea(data-reactid="4").
                  p
                    :markdown-it(inline) **BOLD TEXT**
                  
                  p.
                    In the midst of a large amount of plain
                    text, suddenly a wild #[:markdown-it(inline) *Markdown*]
                    appeared.
            div.col-lg-6(data-reactid="5")
              div(data-reactid="6")
                textarea(readonly data-reactid="7").
                  <p><strong>BOLD TEXT</strong></p>
                  <p>In the midst of a large amount of plain
                    text, suddenly a wild <em>Markdown</em>
                    appeared.</p>
        h2#filtered-includes
          | Filtered Includes
          a.header-anchor(href="#filtered-includes" aria-hidden="true") ¶
        p
          | You can also apply filters to external files, using the
          a(href="includes.html#including-filtered-text") include syntax
          | .
        h2#nested-filters
          | Nested Filters
          a.header-anchor(href="#nested-filters" aria-hidden="true") ¶
        p You can apply multiple filters on the same block of text. To do so, simply specify the filters on the same line.
        p The filters are applied in reverse order. The text is first passed to the last filter; then, the result is passed to the second last filter, and so on.
        p
          | In the following example, the script is first transformed by
          code babel
          | , and then by
          code cdata-js
          | .
        div.preview-wrapper(data-control="interactive" data-index="2")
          div.row(data-reactroot data-reactid="1" data-react-checksum="-685458541")
            div.col-lg-6(data-reactid="2")
              div(data-reactid="3")
                textarea(data-reactid="4").
                  script
                    :cdata-js:babel(presets=['es2015'])
                      const myFunc = () => `This is ES2015 in a CD${'ATA'}`;
            div.col-lg-6(data-reactid="5")
              div(data-reactid="6")
                textarea(readonly data-reactid="7").
                  <script>
                    //<![CDATA[
                    'use strict';
                  
                    var myFunc = function myFunc() {
                      return 'This is ES2015 in a CD' + 'ATA';
                    };
                    //]]>
                  </script>
        h2#custom-filters
          | Custom Filters
          a.header-anchor(href="#custom-filters" aria-hidden="true") ¶
        p
          | You can add your own filters to Pug via the
          a(href="../api/reference.html#options")
            code filters
            | option
          | .
        div
          div.row
            div.col-lg-6
              pre.cm-s-default
                code
                  span.cm-variable options
                  | .
                  span.cm-property filters
                  span.cm-operator =
                  | {
                  span.cm-string.cm-property 'my-own-filter'
                  | :
                  span.cm-keyword function
                  | (
                  span.cm-def text
                  | ,
                  span.cm-def options
                  | ) {
                  span.cm-keyword if
                  | (
                  span.cm-variable-2 options
                  | .
                  span.cm-property addStart
                  | )
                  span.cm-variable-2 text
                  span.cm-operator =
                  span.cm-string 'Start\n'
                  span.cm-operator +
                  span.cm-variable-2 text
                  | ;
                  span.cm-keyword if
                  | (
                  span.cm-variable-2 options
                  | .
                  span.cm-property addEnd
                  | )
                  span.cm-variable-2 text
                  span.cm-operator =
                  span.cm-variable-2 text
                  span.cm-operator +
                  span.cm-string '\nEnd'
                  | ;
                  span.cm-keyword return
                  span.cm-variable-2 text
                  | ; } };
              pre.cm-s-default
                code
                  span.cm-tag p
                  span.cm-indent
                  span.cm-atom :my-own-filter
                  span.cm-punctuation (
                  span.cm-attribute addStart addEnd
                  span.cm-punctuation )
                  span.cm-string Filter
                  span.cm-string Body
            div.col-lg-6
              pre.cm-s-default
                code
                  span.cm-tag.cm-bracket <
                  span.cm-tag p
                  span.cm-tag.cm-bracket >
                  | Start Filter Body End
                  span.cm-tag.cm-bracket </
                  span.cm-tag p
                  span.cm-tag.cm-bracket >
        div(style="margin-top: 50px")
